  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 212 PM MDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nGenerally tranquil weather will persist through much of the long-\nterm period. Large-scale ridging will dominate the weather pattern \nover the western CONUS resulting in generally mild above-average \ntemperatures and limited precipitation chances over southeast \nWyoming and western Nebraska. This ridging pattern is, broadly \nspeaking, a general result of the highly active tropical pattern \nover the eastern Pacific. As the firehose of EPAC tropical storms \nmove west and then recurve to the north in the mid-latitudes of the \ncentral Pacific, potent synoptic troughing is noted over the Bearing \nSea/Gulf of Alaska which thus drives downstream ridging over western \nNorth America. These types of stable-synoptic-wave patterns can \noften persist for weeks or more, locking in high-confidence \nforecasts over much of the west including our region. \n\nRegarding local details in the forecast, expect above-average temps \nfrom Friday onward. High confidence exists in temps around 5 degrees \nabove average this weekend (low to mid-70s east of the mountains) \nand highs 10 to 15 degrees above average through next week. Despite \nthe drier weather in place late this week and through the weekend, \nrising heights should result in overnight lows remaining above \naverage as well. This should hold off any threats of frosts (or \nfreezes) for any populated area of southeast Wyoming outside of the \nmountains. Afternoon RH values will drop to near or slightly below \n20% throughout the weekend during the afternoon hours across all of \nsoutheast Wyoming and portions of the Nebraska panhandle. However, \ngenerally light surface flow out of the west less than 20 mph will \nlikely preclude the need for any fire weather headlines. Overall, \ngiven the synoptic pattern described earlier in this discussion, \nforecast confidence is quite high throughout the entire long-term \nperiod with limited impacts expected.",
